Z||000004||jch82||10-05-2001||03:10 AM||||Nope everything was custom made...   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>If you send me your interior parts for a core charge along with $300, I can have them "made"   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>Like I said, these are very custom. Wouldn't recommend trying it!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>Josh   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>LOL, its been a long night... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jch82_1.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by jch82 (edited 10-05-2001).]||||reg||12
Z||000005||chester||10-05-2001||03:17 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fiero5:<BR><B>I actually did the steering wheel myself. A local leather goods shop has a spray dye for leather that is available in different colors. I masked off the parts of the wheel I didn't want colored red, and sprayed away.<P>Now I think I will do something similar to my Fiero1. The top section of that leather steering wheel would look awesome in white, like Bob's Gargoyle wheel is but his is red.<P>Steve</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Steve,<P>Could you pick up a can of the leather dye for me in red and bring it with you on Sunday.  Would really appreciate it!<P>Rob D.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Fiero5||10-05-2001||07:21 PM||||The steering wheel was sprayed with that spray dye as I mentioned earlier. In fact, I stopped by today and got the white spray dye and did my Fiero1 steering wheel this evening. The other interior pieces were spray painted with a high temp high gloss bright red paint. I like the high temp paint because I get less orange peel effect on the first few coats.<P>Rob, I will try to get up to that store tomm and pick you up a can of red to bring with us to the Stratham NH show on Sunday. <P>They also had burgundy, black, white, pink, yellow, grey, green and blue of the ones I can remember. I wonder if Mr. Hot Rod Phil would be interested in doing parts of his steering wheel in yellow?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>As soon as I have pics of Fiero1' s steering wheel, I will post them.<P>Steve||||reg||7
Z||000007||sloth85GT||10-06-2001||02:55 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fiero5:<BR><B>The other interior pieces were spray painted with a high temp high gloss bright red paint. I like the high temp paint because I get less orange peel effect on the first few coats.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>About that, how exactly did you go about it. Did you scuff them first with sandpaper? How many coats? Did you use a clear coat? How's it holding up?<P><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.mts.net/~kuzma/fieronl.gif">     <BR>Dan<BR>1985 Silver GT||||reg||1
Z||000008||Fiero5||10-06-2001||04:28 PM||||The paint took about 3 coats. I didn't sand them down or anything. I just washed them real good to get any Armor All, oil from fingers and stuff off them. That way the paint wouldn't orange peel too bad on the first few coats and stay smooth.<P>When they were all done and the paint had dried, I then gave them 3 seperate coats of Plasti-kote fast dry Super Lacquer clear coat. That stuff is awesome!<P>The pieces seem to be holding up great. Even the steering wheel with all the driving I have been doing has held up like new. It feels really smooth and looks so cool, but it still keeps the leather feel and grip.<P>The red high temp paint cost me like $5. The lacquer clear coat was the same cost. The red spray dye was $7. Some labor added to that and the finished look looks like I spent many hundreds of dollars<P>Steve||||reg||6
Z||000009||Capt Fiero||10-07-2001||04:42 AM||||I had similar red panels on my car.  Never did the steering wheel though, just all the panels in red and did the dash and such in black.   I eventually got tired of the red and did a spare set of panels in white, then got tired of the white and got a 3rd set of panels from an 88 Formula with the "carbon fiber" look-a-like and just put them in the way they were.  I have found that Plasti-kote brand spray paint and or die (cant remember) really worked the best.  It gives everything a powdercoat like shine and feel.  ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Fiero5||10-08-2001||11:22 AM||||I added a few extra touches since those pics above for the Stratham NH show that was yesterday. The show was great though a bit windy/chilly. Brrrr.<BR>All in  all, it was a great day!<BR>I will have a few pics up unless Chester (hint - hint) or someone else doesn't get some up first? As soon as I get a chance to firewire the digi pics into I-movie to get the stills picked out, I will get some up and some great shots up of the Stratham NH show.<P>Steve||||reg||7
